msgid ""
"[[quarkus-security-webauthn_quarkus-webauthn-new-cookie-interval]] [.property-path]##link:#quarkus-security-webauthn_quarkus-webauthn-new-cookie-interval[`+++quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval+++`]##\n"
"config_property_copy_button:+++quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval+++[]\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"[.description]\n"
"--\n"
"How old a cookie can get before it will be replaced with a new cookie with an updated timeout, also referred to as \"renewal-timeout\". Note that smaller values will result in slightly more server load (as new encrypted cookies will be generated more often); however, larger values affect the inactivity timeout because the timeout is set when a cookie is generated. For example if this is set to 10 minutes, and the inactivity timeout is 30m, if a user's last request is when the cookie is 9m old then the actual timeout will happen 21m after the last request because the timeout is only refreshed when a new cookie is generated. That is, no timeout is tracked on the server side; the timestamp is encoded and encrypted in the cookie itself, and it is decrypted and parsed with each request.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Environment variable: env_var_with_copy_button:+++QUARKUS_WEBAUTHN_NEW_COOKIE_INTERVAL+++[]\n"
"--"
msgstr " link:#quarkus-security-webauthn_quarkus-webauthn-new-cookie-interval[quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval] config_property_copy_button:quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval[]"

#, fuzzy
msgid ""
"[[quarkus-security-webauthn_quarkus-webauthn-new-cookie-interval]] [.property-path]##link:#quarkus-security-webauthn_quarkus-webauthn-new-cookie-interval[`+++quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval+++`]##\n"
"config_property_copy_button:+++quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval+++[]"
msgstr " link:#quarkus-security-webauthn_quarkus-webauthn-new-cookie-interval[quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval] config_property_copy_button:quarkus.webauthn.new-cookie-interval[]"

#, fuzzy
msgid "How old a cookie can get before it will be replaced with a new cookie with an updated timeout, also referred to as \"renewal-timeout\". Note that smaller values will result in slightly more server load (as new encrypted cookies will be generated more often); however, larger values affect the inactivity timeout because the timeout is set when a cookie is generated. For example if this is set to 10 minutes, and the inactivity timeout is 30m, if a user's last request is when the cookie is 9m old then the actual timeout will happen 21m after the last request because the timeout is only refreshed when a new cookie is generated. That is, no timeout is tracked on the server side; the timestamp is encoded and encrypted in the cookie itself, and it is decrypted and parsed with each request."
msgstr "Qual é o tempo máximo de validade de um cookie antes que ele seja substituído por um novo cookie com um tempo de expiração atualizado, também conhecido como “renewal-timeout”. Observe que valores menores resultarão em uma carga um pouco maior no servidor (já que novos cookies criptografados serão gerados com mais frequência); no entanto, valores maiores afetam o tempo limite de inatividade, pois esse tempo limite é definido no momento em que o cookie é gerado. Por exemplo, se esse valor estiver definido como 10 minutos e o tempo limite de inatividade for de 30m, caso a última solicitação de um usuário tenha ocorrido quando o cookie já tinha 9m, o tempo limite efetivo ocorrerá 21m após a última solicitação, pois o tempo limite só é atualizado quando um novo cookie é gerado. Ou seja, nenhum tempo limite é rastreado no lado do servidor; o carimbo de data e hora é codificado e criptografado no próprio cookie, e é descriptografado e analisado a cada solicitação."

